I’d like to talk about a child I know who’s really into drawing, and that’s my cousin Minh, who’s nine years old. I’ve known him since he was born, and I’ve always noticed that he has a strong interest in drawing. He’s the kind of kid who always has a pencil or some coloured pencils nearby, and whenever he has a piece of paper, he’ll usually start drawing something.

What really stands out about Minh is his imagination. He doesn’t just copy pictures from books or cartoons. Instead, he comes up with his own characters and stories. For example, he might draw a superhero with animal features, or an imaginary city floating in the sky. Sometimes, he even tells me the story behind his drawings while he’s working on them, which makes the whole thing more interesting.

As for how often he draws, I’d say almost every day, especially after school and at weekends. He can easily spend an hour or two sketching and colouring without getting bored. In fact, he can become so absorbed in drawing that he sometimes forgets about everything else around him.

I remember that last month, he drew a picture of our family having a picnic. What surprised me was the number of small details he included, such as our facial expressions, the way we were sitting, and even some funny gestures. I probably wouldn’t have noticed some of these things myself, but he somehow managed to capture them.

I think he likes drawing mainly because it gives him a way to express his imagination. He’s quite creative, but he isn’t always very talkative, so drawing seems to be a comfortable way for him to express his ideas and feelings. He also seems to enjoy the process itself, rather than worrying about whether his drawings are perfect.

So, for him, drawing is more than just a hobby. It’s a way to be creative, express himself, and simply have fun in his free time.
